<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Fourier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1772">1772</a>–<a href="/years/1837">1837</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as François Marie Charles Fourier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Fourier was a French philosopher regarded as the founder of utopian socialism. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besancon">Besançon</a>, he worked for years as a travelling salesman and correspondence clerk; his first book, the Theory of the Four Movements, appeared anonymously in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> in 1808 and sold few copies. He held that cooperation was the key to social success and proposed communities called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanxes</a>, housed in buildings called phalanstères, where jobs were assigned by interest and desire. He also called trade, which he associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, the source of all evil. His ideas inspired intentional communities such as Brook Farm and the North American Phalanx.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-fourier.jpg" alt="Charles Fourier" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Gigoux · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_painting_of_Charles_Fourier_by_Jean_Gigoux_—_1835_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>François Marie Charles Fourier was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besancon">Besançon</a> on 7 April 1772, the son of a small businessman. He cared more for architecture than for his father's trade and as a boy was most interested in geography. When his father died in 1781 he received two-fifths of an estate valued at more than 200,000 francs, which allowed him to travel through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. From 1791 to 1816 he worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, Marseille and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> as a travelling salesman and correspondence clerk, complaining of serving the knavery of merchants. He wrote most of his works between 1816 and 1821 and died in Paris on 10 October 1837.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His first book, the Theory of the Four Movements and the General Destinies, appeared anonymously in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> in 1808 and sold few copies. Fourier held that cooperation is the key to social success. People would live in communities called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanxes</a>, housed in phalanstères, four-storey apartment complexes where the richest had the upper floors. Jobs would be assigned by interest and desire, with higher pay for unattractive work. He counted 12 common passions and 810 types of character, so that an ideal phalanx would have 1,620 people. The phalanstères remained concepts, since he never obtained the money to build them.</p><p>Fourier argued that important jobs should be open to women by skill, and he never married. He saw poverty rather than inequality as the chief cause of disorder. He also wrote of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> as the ruin of the body politic, called trade, which he associated with them, the source of all evil, and proposed that Jews be made to farm in the phalansteries. John K. Roth and Richard L. Rubenstein see his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">antisemitism</a> as economic and religious, not the racial kind that emerged later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Fourier's proposals inspired intentional communities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, among them Utopia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, the North American Phalanx in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a> and Brook Farm in Massachusetts, and the Familistery of Guise in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His follower Victor Considerant carried his ideas into the 1848 Revolution and the Paris Commune. Friedrich Engels named him one of three major utopian socialists, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-kropotkin">Peter Kropotkin</a> saw him as founder of the libertarian branch of socialism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles Fourier” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Charles Fourier</b> is credited with coining “féminisme” in 1837, but no trace of the word has been found in his works.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/feminism">Feminism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The first modern currents, later called utopian socialism, are associated with Henri de Saint-Simon, <b>Charles Fourier</b> and Robert Owen.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/socialism">Socialism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besancon">Besançon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.24,6.02&amp;year=1814" aria-label="Besançon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Charles Fourier"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5349 6066h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5349 6066h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.24,6.02&amp;year=1814">What was here in 1814? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
